"Inside a Planet | VirtualDemos",
"Inside a Planet",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Layers: Earth: core and mantle; Earth if it were rock all through; Mercury: a huge core; Venus; Mars; Moon: a tiny core. Convection: Earth: moving plates; Venus: one stiff lid; Venus: a lid that holds, then breaks; Mars: plumes that stay put; Rock equally runny hot or cold; Heated only from inside; Benchmark: one steady roll. Floating crust: Continent and ocean floor; A high plateau with a deep root; A stretched, thin continent.",
"The demo has three parts, chosen at the top of the picture: Layers, Convection and Floating crust. Each shows the planet cut in half in 3D, or a flat drawing.",
"<strong>Layers</strong> builds a planet from two layers, an iron core and a rock mantle, each with one density. The core’s radius and the two densities can be changed. The readouts give the model planet’s mean density and its moment of inertia factor C/MR² next to the values measured for the real planet, and the share of the volume and of the mass that is in the core. The graph shows density against distance from the center; for Earth it also shows PREM, the layers found from earthquake waves.",
"<strong>Convection</strong> runs a real calculation of rock flowing in half of a planet’s mantle, heated by the core below and by radioactive decay inside, and cooled at the surface. Colors show the temperature, white arrows the flow, and orange arrows outside the rim how fast the surface itself moves. The readouts give the time in the model in millions of years (Myr), the surface speed in cm per year, the heat coming out of the top compared with a mantle that does not move, the mantle’s mean temperature, the number of rising plumes and whether the lid moves. Start runs it; changing a setting starts the mantle again.",
"<strong>Floating crust</strong> floats a block of continent and a block of ocean floor on the mantle and gives how high each stands and how deep its root goes.",
"Record reading stores the readouts of the part on show. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the matching Data table below; Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table.",
"Simplifications: the layers each have one density (real rock and iron are squeezed denser with depth); the convection is in two dimensions and runs far less vigorously than a real mantle, so its plates move more slowly than Earth’s; the floating crust leaves out the ocean’s water.",
"Find out what is inside the rocky planets: use a planet’s mean density and how its mass is spread to weigh its iron core, then see how heat escaping from inside moves the rock, and why continents stand high.",
"<strong>Density.</strong> Density is mass divided by volume. A planet’s mass comes from the orbits of its moons or of spacecraft, and its volume from its radius, so its mean density is known. Rocks at the surface of Earth have densities of about {#} to {#} kg/m³; pure iron, before it is squeezed, {#} kg/m³. If a planet is denser on average than its surface rocks, something denser must be inside.",
"<strong>How the mass is spread.</strong> A spinning planet’s moment of inertia says how far from its center its mass sits. Astronomers write it as a number C/MR², which can be measured from the way the planet’s spin axis slowly wobbles. A ball of the same density all through has C/MR² = {#}. If the mass is crowded toward the middle, as in a planet with a heavy core, the number is smaller than {#}.",
"<strong>Differentiation.</strong> When the young planets were hot enough to melt, heavy iron sank to the middle and lighter rock floated up. Planets that did this are <em>differentiated</em>: an iron core, a rock mantle and a thin crust on top.",
"<strong>A churning mantle.</strong> The mantle is solid rock, yet over millions of years it flows like very thick syrup. Heated from below by the core and from inside by radioactive elements, hot rock rises and cold rock sinks: convection. The cold rock at the top is stiff. On Earth it breaks into plates that move and sink back into the mantle. On Venus it seems to stay one unbroken shell, a <em>stagnant lid</em>. On Mars, hot rock rising under a lid that never moves built the giant volcanoes of Tharsis.",
"<strong>Floating crust.</strong> Over millions of years the mantle flows enough that blocks of crust float on it like ice on water. A block sinks until it pushes aside its own weight of mantle; the lighter and thicker the block, the higher it stands.",
"Earth’s mean density is {#} kg/m³. The mantle rock at its surface is about {#} kg/m³. If Earth were made of that rock all through, what would its mean density be? What does the difference tell you?",
"Mars’s mean density is {#} kg/m³ and its C/MR² is {#}. The Moon’s are {#} kg/m³ and {#}. Which of the two do you expect to have the bigger core for its size? Why?",
"Ice floats with about {#}/{#} of it under water. A block of crust of density {#} kg/m³ floats on mantle rock of density {#} kg/m³. What fraction of the block do you expect to lie below the mantle’s level?",
"<strong>Part {#}: weigh the core of Mars.</strong> Choose Layers and the Mars setup. Keep its core density and mantle density as they are. Record a reading with the core radius at {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} km. Send the readings to the worksheet (they fill the first Data table), then clear them.",
"<strong>Part {#}: five worlds.</strong> Choose each of the setups Mercury, Venus, Earth (core and mantle), Mars and Moon in turn, and record one reading of each without changing anything. Then choose Earth if it were rock all through and record a reading. Send the six readings to the worksheet (they fill the second Data table), then clear them.",
"<strong>Part {#}: plates or a lid.</strong> Choose Convection and the setup Earth: moving plates, and start it ({#} steps a frame makes it run faster). When the clock passes {#} Myr, record a reading; record two more at about {#} and {#} Myr. Do the same for Venus: one stiff lid and Mars: plumes that stay put. While each runs, watch the orange arrows at the surface and where the hot rock rises. Send the nine readings to the worksheet (they fill the third Data table), then clear them.",
